{"n":56,"paragraphs":["People everywhere have always believed in the supernatural, and Swamiji chose to approach the subject through things he had seen for himself.","He once heard of a man who could answer questions you only carried in your head, and who could also predict the future. Curious, Swamiji went with a few friends. To be careful, each wrote his questions down and kept the paper in his pocket. The moment the man saw them, he repeated their questions and answered them. He then wrote something on a slip, folded it, had Swamiji sign the back, and told him to keep it unread in his pocket. He did the same for each of them, and also told them of things that would happen to them later.","Next he asked each to think of any word or sentence in any language. Swamiji chose a long Sanskrit sentence, a language the man did not know. When he opened his paper, that very sentence was written there, with a note that he would think of it. A friend thought of a Koran passage in Arabic and found it on his slip too. Another, a doctor, chose a line from a German medical book, and it was also there. Thinking he might have been fooled, Swamiji returned days later with different friends. Once again the man succeeded completely.","In Hyderabad he met a respected Brahmin businessman said to make things appear from nowhere. The man had a fever and asked Swamiji to place a hand on his head, since a holy man's touch was believed to heal. Swamiji agreed, on condition that he show his skill. Later the Brahmin returned. He was stripped to a loincloth, wrapped in Swamiji's own blanket and seated in a corner while twenty-five people watched. They wrote down fruits that did not grow in that region, and out from under the blanket came bunches of grapes, oranges and more, heavier than the man himself. Some feared hypnotism, but he ate first and they all ate. Finally he produced heaps of perfect roses, dew still on the petals. Asked how, he only said it was sleight of hand, which hardly seemed possible.","Swamiji admitted there is plenty of fraud in such matters. But fraud is an imitation, and you cannot imitate what does not exist. Long ago Indian thinkers studied these happenings, found that they follow natural laws like anything else, and turned them into a science, Raja-yoga, that can be practised and learned. Their conclusion was that all such extraordinary powers lie in the human mind."],"learn":"Swamiji didn't swallow claims blindly: he tested them, repeated the test, and then looked for the principle behind them. Curiosity plus careful checking is a good habit for any field. And the bigger point holds: the trained mind has far more potential than we usually use.","source":"Vol. 2, pp. 10–12","images":["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]}